// keybindings.windows.json — a sample Windows/Linux-flavored tecode user
// keybindings file (Issue #115, replacing the removed "windows"
// keybindings.preset). Copy this to ~/.config/tecode/keybindings.json (or
// %APPDATA%\tecode\keybindings.json on Windows), or merge these entries
// into your own file — same plain JSONC `{ key, command, when? }` shape
// documented in samples/keybindings.json.
//
// Deliberately small: this codebase's defaults are already
// VS-Code-on-Windows/Linux-shaped throughout (ctrl-based shortcuts
// everywhere), so there is little left to change. The one real divergence
// in the shipped defaults is editor-core's move/duplicate-line bindings,
// which use alt+meta+up / alt+meta+down / shift+alt+meta+down — a
// macOS idiom (meta = Cmd; see packages/builtin/editor-core/manifest.ts's
// own TSDoc for why an Alt-held arrow always carries BOTH the alt and
// meta tokens on this terminal input pipeline). VS Code's real
// Windows/Linux defaults for the same three actions omit the Cmd/meta
// modifier entirely: alt+up / alt+down / shift+alt+down. The three
// entries below ADD those keys for the same three commands — the
// meta-flavored defaults stay bound too, harmlessly unreachable on a real
// Windows/Linux terminal since there's no meta key to hold — rather than
// removing anything, so nothing else needs to change.
[
  { "key": "alt+up", "command": "editor.action.moveLinesUp", "when": "editorTextFocus" },
  { "key": "alt+down", "command": "editor.action.moveLinesDown", "when": "editorTextFocus" },
  { "key": "shift+alt+down", "command": "editor.action.duplicateLine", "when": "editorTextFocus" }
]
